  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails basic accessibility design requirements for an interactive disclosure widget. It uses a non-interactive `<div>` as the trigger, lacks keyboard support, omits all required ARIA states/properties per the WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, and exposes visual-only state indicators to assistive technology. This will completely block keyboard users from interacting with the component and leave screen reader users without any programmatic indication of expanded/collapsed state.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reading code, I predicted: (1) `<div>` used as interactive trigger instead of `<button>`, (2) missing `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`, (3) no keyboard event handling (`onKeyDown` for Enter/Space), (4) visual `+`/`\u2212` symbols exposed to AT without `aria-hidden`. All predictions materialized during investigation.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. Non-interactive element used as interactive control. See `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:8` where `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>` replaces a native `<button>`. Screen readers do not announce `<div>` elements as buttons, and keyboard users cannot focus or activate it via Tab/Enter/Space. Per WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) and 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), interactive controls must use native HTML semantics. Fix: Replace `<div>` with `<button type=\"button\">` and move `onClick` to `onToggle` or keep as `onClick` on the button.\n\n2. Missing keyboard activation handler. See `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:8-12`. Even if focus is manually placed on the trigger, there is no `onKeyDown` listener for `Enter` or `Space`. Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern and WCAG 2.1.1, custom interactive widgets must support standard keyboard activation keys. Fix: Add `onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') { e.preventDefault(); toggle(); } }}` to the trigger element.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. Missing `aria-expanded` state communication. See `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:8`. The component tracks `isExpanded` in React state but never exposes it to the accessibility tree. Screen reader users hear the title but have no programmatic way to know if content is expanded or collapsed. Per WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern and WCAG 4.1.2, toggle buttons must expose `aria-expanded=\"true\"` or `\"false\"`. Fix: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the trigger element.\n\n2. Missing `aria-controls` / ID association between trigger and panel. See `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:8` and `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:14`. The trigger does not reference the content panel, and the panel lacks an `id`. Screen reader users cannot programmatically understand the parent-child relationship. Per WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) and WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, the button must have `aria-controls=\"panel-id\"` and the panel must have `id=\"panel-id\"`. Fix: Assign `id=\"expandable-content-1\"` to the content `<div>` and `aria-controls=\"expandable-content-1\"` to the trigger.\n\n3. Visual state symbols (`+`/`\u2212`) exposed to assistive technology without suppression. See `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10`. Screen readers will announce \"plus\" or \"minus\" as literal text, which is redundant and confusing when combined with missing ARIA state announcements. Per WCAG 4.1.2, decorative or programmatic-only indicators must be hidden from AT. Fix: Wrap the symbols in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- Missing focus indicator styling on the trigger. If converted to a `<button>`, it will inherit browser defaults, but custom CSS classes often strip them. Ensure `:focus-visible` styles are applied per WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible).\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Consider adding `aria-label` or `aria-describedby` if the trigger text alone is insufficient in context (e.g., multiple expandables on one page). \n- Add `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` to the content panel for better landmark navigation.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Native HTML semantics: `<button>` is entirely absent; ARIA is not used at all, meaning the component relies solely on visual cues and mouse interaction.\n- Keyboard event handling: No `onKeyDown` implementation for Enter/Space activation.\n- Programmatic state communication: Zero ARIA attributes (`aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`) despite tracking state in React.\n- Element association: No `id`/`aria-controls` pairing between trigger and content panel.\n- Symbol suppression: Visual `+`/`\u2212` indicators leak into the accessibility tree.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears only the title text, followed by \"plus\" or \"minus\". No indication the element is clickable, expandable, or collapsed. Cannot determine state without visual inspection.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Cannot Tab to the trigger (it's a `<div>`). Even if focus is forced via devtools, Enter/Space does nothing. Complete interaction blockage.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom)**: Relies on visual styling (`cursor: pointer`, color changes) to infer interactivity. If CSS is stripped or contrast fails, the component becomes invisible to interaction.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Interaction model is ambiguous. Without explicit \"Expand\" / \"Collapse\" labels or clear ARIA state, users with cognitive load differences may not understand how to toggle content or what the current state is.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails at the foundational level of accessible interaction design. It violates native HTML-first principles, omits all required WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern attributes, and provides zero keyboard support. This is not a styling or minor attribute gap; it is a complete interaction model failure for assistive technology users. REJECT until core semantics, ARIA state communication, and keyboard activation are implemented.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Will this component be instantiated multiple times on a page? If so, `id` values must be dynamically generated or scoped to avoid duplicate ID collisions in the DOM.\n- Is `content` potentially long-form? If so, consider adding `aria-describedby` to the trigger pointing to a brief description of what will expand, per WCAG 1.3.1.",
